Good advice also, but...

Get three Irishmen in a room, they'll form seven
political parties. Seems like the same is true of
seeking programming methodology advice.

Integrated development environments (IDEs) are
convenient.  They are splendid and helpful tools.

They are also crutches, and like crutches, can help
you when/while you have trouble getting around, but
can also slow you down and leave you convinced with
long use that a cripple is all you are able to be.

Deciding to use one is a decision fraught with
consequences for both ill and weal.

        I've seen a set of seven programming teams
        where one team's poor tool choice left it
        constantly late to meet deadlines, while the
        other six teams had far fewer problems. The
        issue? The one team's task size had outgrown
        its overly complex tool choice, and the tool
        (Clearcase, a configuration management tool)
        spent more time fighting them than assisting
        them.

In your case, NetHack, despite its apparent
overwhelming complexity to the newcomer, is really,
in the world of commercial programming, a tiny
programming application that, once installed into an
IDE, should utterly underwhelm most IDEs [but the
"plug", into the IDE of an existing software suite
like NetHack, of "plug and play simplicity" is no
simple matter in itself].

        The above-described team programming task
        (Motorola's IRIDIUM satellite and
        telecommunications software), in contrast,
        was anything but "tiny".

        It involved some one hundred plus million
        lines of source code (most of it bought via
        source code licensing rather than code built
        in-house, but all of it compiled from
        scratch with each new release (to cater for
        various software build tool upgrades and
        licensed source code upgrades and patches
        always ongoing in such a huge application)
        to make the final "product kit"). [I worked
        as a member of the build team toward the end
        of the project for a few months, but as an
        application programmer most of my time there
        working on network management software.]

